ncluding ravens, coyotes, bears, and invertebrates, benefit from or consume remains of wolf kills.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills. For example, when wolves kill an elk, ravens and magpies arrive almost immediately. Coyotes arrive soon after, waiting nearby until the wolves are sated. Bears will attempt to chase the wolves away, and are usually successful. Many other animals—from eagles to invertebrates—consume the remains.","narrative":"Laat zien dat een wolvendoding voedsel biedt aan dieren van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source supports each named group benefiting from or consuming wolf-kill remains. “Delen in het voedsel” does not imply intentional sharing.","materiality":"Low."},{"excerpt":"De samenstelling van de prooien veranderde eveneens.","claim":"The composition of wolves’ prey changed over time.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"De veranderde samenstelling van hun prooi in verschillende perioden maakt concreet dat predatiepatronen veranderen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"none","explanation":"The source documents a change in prey composition across the stated periods.","materiality":"Low."},{"excerpt":"Onder de prooien die in de vroege winter werden gedood, verschoof tussen 1995–2000 en 2001–2007 de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden.","claim":"Among early-winter wolf prey, the relative proportions of elk calves and bull elk changed between 1995–2000 and 2001–2007.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"Gebruik daarnaast 